# Paper flow (v5) — when does *where* beat *how many*?

**v1** measurement, no method. **v2** protocol-as-contribution — rejected, correctly. **v3** method
section reporting a negative. **v4** conditional method that works on V\*Bench. **v5 adds the transfer
test, and the transfer test changes the claim**: allocation-vs-budget is **benchmark-dependent**, and
the paper's job is to say when each wins.

---

## The one-sentence claim

> At a **matched realized token budget**, *where* a VLM spends visual tokens can dominate *how many*
> — by ≥26× on V\*Bench, across four architectures. But this **does not hold universally**: on
> HR-Bench 4k the budget axis is far from saturated and doubling tokens beats reallocating them. We
> give a training-free conditional allocator that captures **32.9% of the oracle gain** where
> allocation pays, show **which half of it transfers** and which does not, and identify the
> saturation condition that separates the two regimes.

**This is a weaker headline than v4 and a more defensible paper.** A conditional claim with a
measured boundary beats an unconditional claim that a second benchmark contradicts.

---

## §1 — Problem: nobody holds realized tokens fixed

Pruning holds LLM-side tokens fixed; LLMind/Gizdov hold pixels; ViRGo holds seconds; crop/zoom
(V\*/SEAL, ZoomEye, DeepEyes, AwaRes, VisionThink) hold nothing and let tokens grow. **Nobody holds
realized tokens fixed and moves them.**

**Instrument.** Arms calibrated by iterating on the processor's own `image_grid_thw` until realized
merged-token counts match; a **budget gate** voids any contrast >10% off. Counts are always
*measured* — computing them once faked an entire phase.

---

## §2 — Where allocation wins: the exchange rate on V\*Bench

n=191, Qwen3-VL-2B, budget-matched:

| arm | tokens | acc |
|---|---|---|
| `crop_only@300` | 300 | **93.7%** |
| `uniform@300` | 294 | 56.5% |
| `uniform@8000` | 7957 | 84.8% |

Placement at 300 beats uniform at 7957 — **no crossing at 26.5×**. `crop_random@300` = 38.2%, below
baseline, so the effect is placement, not cropping.

**Four architectures** (placement effect): Qwen2-VL **+59.2pp**, Qwen3-VL +55.5pp, onevision
**+49.2pp**, LLaVA-NeXT **+42.4pp**.

### §2.1 Two corrections
- Bare test multiples are not comparable: dynamic ranges differ 1000× (Qwen2-VL 1944×, LLaVA-NeXT
  **1.5×**). onevision's 3.9× is ~67% of all it can express; Qwen's 26× is 1–21% of theirs.
  LLaVA-NeXT cannot run the sweep at all (one rung), and its budget row is **VOID** (14% off gate).
- **All of §2 is one benchmark.** §5 is why that matters.

---

## §3 — Mechanism: sub-token evidence, not routed

- **71% of V\*Bench targets occupy <1 merged token** at B₀=300 (median 0.32).
- C3 dose-response: error **32.8% → 1.0%** across seven strata (n=3387).
- On failures the evidence **does not reach the answer position** (Phase 26, answer-matched).
- Threshold retuning refuted (0.2pp).

⇒ **C2 is a routing failure**, claimed on Phase 26 alone.

### §3.1 Retraction
An earlier draft also claimed the target is **encoded at its own visual tokens even when the model
errs** (`obj` probe, AUROC **1.000**). **Withdrawn.** The probe read a **GT box for positives** and a
**random rectangle for negatives**, so it could win by detecting region *type* — query-independent.

| control | result |
|---|---|
| C1 region-type probe (positives only) | **0.964–0.971, flat, L0 = 0.967** |
| C2 ROI-matched, wrong cohort | 0.742 vs null 0.600 |
| C2 ROI-matched, all items | 0.599 vs null 0.532 |
| C3 geometry-only, no hidden states | 0.629 |

**0.967 at layer 0** — the shortcut lives in the raw embeddings, before the LLM computes anything.
The surviving residual is small, peaks at L0, and (since the matched block reads a *random* region)
supports only "presence is weakly decodable from **arbitrary** tokens," not local encoding. A
label-independent ROI is unobtainable on POPE; that is a probe **design** problem, left open.

> **Rule earned**, after two circularity defects in one phase: **an AUROC near 1.000 is a bug report
> until a matched control says otherwise.**

---

## §4 — Method

### §4.1 The localizer
Phase 22 pre-registered that attention selects *against* small targets — **refuted**: strongest
retention at the smallest stratum (+34.8pp). Replicated on a second benchmark: GT cell at the
**16th percentile** vs exact chance 50th.

The read-out matters more than the layer: min/max bbox is **DEAD** (median area 1.000 everywhere) —
attention is concentrated but **not co-located**, with positionally-fixed corner sinks holding the
top ranks. Mean over **layers 16–26** + outer-ring mask + argmax gives **gt_pct 0.034,
argmax-in-GT 17.3% = 157× chance**, selection-free and data-free.

### §4.2 The fixed policy fails
Held-out 60.2% vs uniform 56.5% (**CI includes zero**), vs random-window 37.7% (**+22.5pp,
CI [+13.6,+31.9]**). **FAILS the compute-matched bar (66.0%)**; capture 10.0%. Placement does real
work; there is not enough of it.

**Why:** cropping around one peak **destroys inter-object layout** — `direct_attributes` +11.3pp,
`relative_position` **−7.9pp**; smallest quartile +19.1pp, largest **−10.4pp**.

### §4.3 The conditional allocator
Two rules, no fitted parameter: (1) relational question → don't reallocate; (2) else take whichever
**pass** is more confident. W by 5-fold CV (W=0.15 in every fold).

| gate | CV acc | vs uniform | capture |
|---|---|---|---|
| **text+conf** | **68.6%** | **+12.0pp** [+6.8,+17.3] | **32.9%** |
| conf only | 66.5% | +10.0pp | 27.2% |
| `learned_LR` (**fitted**) | 57.1% | +0.5pp | — |

Clears the compute-matched control; capture sits **inside the published training-free proposer's
31–44%**, with no training or auxiliary model.

**§4.4 Two hand-written rules beat the learned gate by 11.5pp.** At n=191 with ~61 actionable items
there is no signal to fit. The paper's answer to "should allocation be learned?" is **not at this
data scale** — and that is a result, not an excuse.

---

## §5 — The transfer test, and the boundary it exposes

HR-Bench 4k, n=800 rows / 200 instances, 4032×4032 images. **Nothing refitted** — W and both rules
carried over. The V\*Bench confound is genuinely broken here: keyword concordance with the benchmark's
own category label is **53.5%** (chance) vs **99.0%** on V\*Bench.

| arm | per-row | vs uniform | CircularEval |
|---|---|---|---|
| uniform@300 | 52.5% | — | 36.5% |
| **uniform@600** (compute-matched) | **59.2%** | +6.8pp | **45.5%** |
| always attn@0.15 | 43.8% | **−8.8pp** | 27.5% |
| **conf_route@0.15** | **56.1%** | **+3.6pp** [+1.0,+6.2] | **44.5%** |
| text_gate@0.15 | 45.0% | **−7.5pp** | 27.0% |
| text+conf (V\*Bench gate) | 53.4% | +0.9pp [−1.2,+3.0] | 39.0% |

**Verdict (pre-registered): PARTIAL TRANSFER.**

### §5.1 The decomposition is the contribution
- **`conf_route` transfers**: +3.6pp per-row (CI excludes zero), **+8.0pp CircularEval**, nearly
  matching the compute-matched control at equal cost. **This is the portable method.**
- **The text rule does not, and is harmful**: −7.5pp alone; it drags `conf_route` 56.1% → **53.4%**.
  On V\*Bench it was the stronger half. **§4.3's composition is V\*Bench-specific.**

### §5.2 A prediction we made and got wrong
We pre-registered that HR-Bench's *referential* prepositions would make the rule skip items
allocation helps. Measured: **−5.6pp on fired rows, −9.7pp on unfired**. Both negative — allocation
hurts everywhere here and the rule is merely *backwards*. Reported because the prediction was
recorded before running.

### §5.3 The boundary: budget saturation
On V\*Bench, doubling tokens buys +9.5pp and placement still wins at 26×. On HR-Bench 4k, doubling
buys **+6.8pp / +9.0pp circular** while our allocator **loses 8.8pp**. The governing variable appears
to be **how much headroom remains on the budget axis**, which §2.1's dynamic-range measurements make
directly measurable per architecture and per benchmark.

### §5.4 The control that resolves it — the localizer transfers, allocation does not
HR-Bench ships no boxes, so Phase 33 alone could not separate "allocation does not pay at 4K" from
"our localizer fails at 4032x4032". A random-centre window of the **same size** on the **same
instances** settles it:

| arm | per-row | CircularEval |
|---|---|---|
| uniform@300 | 52.5% | 36.5% |
| **attn@0.15** | **43.8%** | **27.5%** |
| **rand@0.15** | **30.1%** | **12.5%** |

    attn - rand = +13.6pp  CI [+10.0, +17.2]     (V*Bench, same contrast: +22.5pp)

**The localizer TRANSFERS** — +13.6pp over random placement at 4K, on a benchmark it was never tuned
on, CI excluding zero. It is now a **two-benchmark** result and the most portable part of the method.
**Allocation still loses** (-8.8pp): the crop finds the target well enough to beat random by a wide
margin, but not well enough to beat seeing the whole image. At W=0.15 a crop is ~605px of a 4032px
source — **44x magnification at the cost of discarding 97.75% of the image.**

So the claim is precise: **reallocating at a fixed budget does not pay at 4K** — and *not* that the
attention localizer fails, which is refuted here.

### §5.5 The boundary condition (the paper's sharpest claim)
| | V\*Bench | HR-Bench 4k |
|---|---|---|
| target size at B₀=300 | **71% sub-token** | resolvable at B₀ |
| doubling the budget buys | +9.5pp | **+6.8pp / +9.0pp circ** |
| allocation at matched budget | **+37.2pp** (oracle) | **−8.8pp** |
| localizer vs random placement | +22.5pp | **+13.6pp** |

> **Allocation pays when the evidence is smaller than the budget can resolve, and costs when it is
> not.** The localizer is good in both regimes; what changes is whether acting on it helps.

This is testable in advance: §2.1's per-architecture floors/ceilings and the sub-token fraction at B₀
are both measurable without running the method.

**Design implication (untested):** W was transferred at 0.15 unadapted — ~337px on V\*Bench's ~2250px
sources, ~605px on HR-Bench's 4032px sources: absolutely larger, relatively identical. A window that
scales with *localizer uncertainty* rather than a constant image fraction is the obvious next design.

## §6 — The same mechanism kills the obvious alternative

Region packing is **not prior art** but is **structurally untestable**: computed from oracle boxes,
so on a relational question the arrangement it reconstructs *is* the answer. V\*Bench's only
multi-box split is entirely relational; HR-Bench has no boxes. What survives: **median 81% of the
union bbox is dead space** (n=54) — a published method spends four-fifths of its budget on nothing.

---

## §7 — What this bounds

| proposer | oracle capture (V\*Bench) |
|---|---|
| fixed policy | 10.0% |
| **conditional (ours, training-free)** | **32.9%** |
| ViRGo RAP (published, training-free) | 31–44% |
| perfect gate over our arms | 47.8% |
| oracle | 100% |

Two rules recover a third of the V\*Bench headroom. The other two-thirds, and the HR-Bench regime,
are open.

---

## §8 — Limitations

1. §2's exchange rate and all four architecture results are **V\*Bench only**; §5 shows a second
   benchmark where the budget axis wins.
2. §5.4's control is now run: "reallocating at a fixed budget does not pay at 4K" IS claimed;
   "the localizer fails" is refuted (+13.6pp over random placement there).
3. §4.2's splits are post-hoc.
4. §4.3's text rule is 99% concordant with the V\*Bench category annotation — and §5 shows it does
   not transfer, which is consistent with it having been annotation-shaped.
5. Allocation arms other than §4.3/§5 are **oracle** (GT boxes).
6. Scale confound: Qwen3-VL is 2B, others 7B; attention results are one model family.
7. §3.1's encoding claim is retracted; a label-independent presence probe remains unbuilt.
8. Whether early-layer attention is a worse *pruning* criterion is **conjecture** — we measured
   localization rank, not pruning quality.

---

## §9 — Related work

Resource-axis table: pruning (LLM tokens fixed); LLMind/Gizdov (pixels); ViRGo (seconds); crop/zoom
incl. AwaRes, DeepEyes, VisionThink (nothing fixed, tokens grow); CARES (learned *how many*);
CROP/AwaRes (learned *where*). **Ours holds realized tokens fixed, moves them, and decides whether to
move them at all** — and reports the regime where that decision is to leave them alone.
